  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Ubin Tegel",
    "overview": "The page is the floor of an old Indonesian house, and each section is a room laid in its own cement tile. Surfaces are matte and square, set edge to edge with a thick ink joint between them. Bottle green is the ground, bone is where reading happens, and ochre, oxblood and indigo appear as tile colour. Chunky soft serif lettering sits against a quiet grotesk for text.\n\nDensity is moderate and structural rather than airy: the first viewport is a grid of tiles (a large bone reading panel, one photograph tile, one pattern tile, a stat strip at the base) and later sections reuse the same module of panels separated by grout. Ornament is authored geometry (four mirror-symmetric tegel motifs) and lives on grounds, dividers, swatch chips and the process floor, not behind body text.\n\nThe build departs from the cream-ground, serif-plus-one-terracotta-accent, equal-card-row pattern. It has no rounded corners and no shadows.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Bottle-green ground (botol-deep) with bone reading panels.",
      "Every corner is square; depth comes from 3px ink grout, not shadow.",
      "Four tegel motifs (kunci, kotak, bintang, daun) generated as SVG data-URIs from one source.",
      "Display serif for lettering, grotesk for text and uppercase labels.",
      "Photographs sit square-cornered in the tile grid on their average-colour ground."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Grout Rule",
        "body": "Ink is the joint colour. Containers take the ink background and their children sit in a 3px gap, so the seams between tiles are drawn by the container, never by individual borders.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Reading Panel Rule",
        "body": "Long text sits on bone in ink, or on green in bone (80% or more opacity). Tegel patterns never sit behind body text.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Serif Rule",
        "body": "Lettering is Young Serif at regular weight; emphasis comes from size and colour, never faux bold or italic.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Floor Rule",
        "body": "A floor has no shadows. Hierarchy comes from panel colour and grout, never blur or lift.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do keep every corner square (0px) and let a container's ink background plus a 3px gap draw the joints.",
      "Do put reading text on bone in ink, or on green in bone at 80% or more opacity.",
      "Do draw tegel motifs from `src/lib/tegel.ts`; add new motifs only as mirror-symmetric shapes in the existing palettes.",
      "Do place photographs as square tiles over their average colour, with an ink joint, and keep alt text from the image slot data.",
      "Do keep the ochre focus ring (2px, 3px offset) and honour reduced motion for every animated entrance."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't use shadows, blur or rounded corners to create depth; the floor is flat.",
      "Don't put a tegel pattern behind body text.",
      "Don't use indigo for text or surfaces; it is tile colour.",
      "Don't set display type in anything but Young Serif at regular weight.",
      "Don't introduce a second accent beside ochre for actions or return to a cream ground."
    ]
  }
}